From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 20:06:24 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] d2d1: Work out what geometries fill, combine and stroke, as
 Windows does.

CombineWithGeometry, Outline, CompareWithGeometry, ComputeLength,
ComputePointAtLength, Widen, GetWidenedBounds and a path's Stream were stubs
for every geometry; so were a group's Simplify, Tessellate, ComputeArea and
contains-point tests, and an ellipse's and a rounded rectangle's GetBounds and
contains-point tests.  Excel draws its charts through CombineWithGeometry.

polygon.c lays two flattened geometries over each other: the edges cut where
they meet, ends within a few float units of each other taken as one vertex,
and each piece knowing which of the two fills either side of it.  Combining
keeps the pieces with the result on one side only and follows them round into
figures; pieces along one curve are put back into a part of that curve, so
curves stay curves.  Widening lays a stroke out as convex pieces -- segments,
joins, caps, round ones drawn as curves -- and puts them together the same
way.  Sorting carries its keys, since geometries are worked on in more than
one thread at once.

What each hands back was measured on Windows (build 29671) with the probes in
wine-altars' tools/d2dgeomprobe, and is matched to the last digit or so:

- Curves are halved until both control points are nearer, across and down,
  than the tolerance to where they would be on the chord.  Areas and lengths
  follow from that; the two ComputeArea tests marked todo_wine now pass, and
  areas are worked out in doubles over what is filled.
- Combining and outlining give the fill mode first, then each figure from its
  topmost, then leftmost, vertex, the figures beginning lowest first; a line
  to each point and back to the start, holes turning the other way.
- Geometries that touch overlap; identical ones are contained.
- The tangent at a length along a curve is between the curve's own at the
  ends of the segment the point is on, each weighted by the part of the curve
  the segment arriving at that end covers, and a curve's own ends by an
  eighteenth.
- A mitre past its limit is cut off square at it, the limit at least one; a
  line turning straight back has its mitre reach straight on.
- Ellipses have lines of no length between their curves, and ellipses and
  rounded rectangles mark every join round, so they widen round; a group sets
  the flags back after a geometry that marked them.  Their bounds are those of
  the curves, worked out in doubles.
- Stream gives back the segments as they were given: lines and cubic curves
  run together, quadratic curves and arcs one at a time, the segment flags
  where they change, an arc to where it starts left out.

On the way: a rounded rectangle's Simplify had its corners upside down; arcs
are halved into quarters as Windows halves them, so three quarters of a
circle are four curves, not three; a path's bounds were those of a quadratic
standing in for each cubic; FillContainsPoint on a path took curves for the
lines between their ends and ignored the tolerance; a stroke without a style
is hit on its joins too; a dash that ends where its figure does takes the
figure's end cap; flattening could halve for ever at a tolerance of zero.

Not as Windows: the figures Widen gives are closed and do not overlap, where
Windows' are open and wind over each other over the same area; a curve
widened is the flattened one widened, so the area differs a little (533.3
against 531.2 for the ellipse in the tests); hollow figures are not handed
through combining.  The tests pass on Windows too, run there as they are.

